{
  "dom_id": "step-t4713",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Suppose ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) are two events in a random experiment.\nThe conditional probability ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/576f25eda2a4f4b8004bee0945ba88362617547b6498cbdc200cb7114a38e0d7.svg) is the probability that the event ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) occurs *given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) has already occurred*.\n\nWe've already seen how to compute conditional probabilities using Venn diagrams. But is there a formula that we can use?\n\nBy definition, we say that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/576f25eda2a4f4b8004bee0945ba88362617547b6498cbdc200cb7114a38e0d7.svg) is given by the formula\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/adaa2c430122663f1249a422ae60292ca1c826971dca0a46798fbb66eb9561bd.svg)\n\nFor instance, let's suppose we have different shaped and colored candies in a bag, and we take one out at random. Let's assume that the probability of picking a green circle candy is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/3a51254ae79153d6f03412adbc61ab4b9289d090a78eee93d6fdf7b095bfb624.svg) while the probability of picking a green candy is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/c6f3ee2917057e25cb4eb459b0bcfbeacc530d386dc2c1c9839a638063cbf801.svg) Then, what is the probability that the candy we have picked is a circle, given that it is green?\n\nLet's call ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) the event \"the candy is a circle\" and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) the event \"the candy is green.\" Then we need to find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/b0e6dab10f3c6515e3378df3672a0752213e3e0a65c68c89c0443fca49473e61.svg)\n\n- The probability of picking a green circle candy is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/3a51254ae79153d6f03412adbc61ab4b9289d090a78eee93d6fdf7b095bfb624.svg) so ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/58570601156b0b580ad6018902eea748ad371578c948962b6060faa28c62af67.svg)\n\n- The probability of picking a green candy is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/19396f6c2d19827db32af5f2d5b135318e08e56751991bcb7f797e96382eca81.svg) therefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/6a7202efcd726bbea2aa6f74ff3b7b5f8f20feeffa98cc49a08dde18c26ffe8c.svg)\n\nSo, using the definition of a conditional probability, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275036/assets/18b1fbf925e7539ebed923bfc567a0e24de0b6d04eba62d9e6cee5f8a4a26e40.svg)",
  "worked_solution": "",
